\score controls the score and the sequencer that plays it.
It does nothing by itself and takes no arguments, you must
call one of its methods, concatenated with a dot, like:

\score.show

To start and stop the sequencer, call:

\score.play
\score.stop
\score.stopnow

\score.stop stops at the start of the next bar, while
\score.stopnow stops immediately. \score.play, \score.stop
and \score.stopnow take no arguments.

\score.finish makes the sequencer stop at the end of the
current loop and marks the end of the score.
\score.reset resets the position of the instruments in
their bars and sets the sequencer to not running.

\score.beatsin sets a countdown of beats before the
score starts playing, for example:

\score.beatsin 4

To show the score or one of its views, call \score.show
with one of the arguments notes, pianoroll, scope, or seq
(or sequencer). Without an argument it shows the notes.

\score.show pianoroll

The piano roll can take its lowest and highest keys, where
c4 is the middle C. The default range is A0 to C8.
Muted instruments are hidden in the piano roll.
Every note is as opaque as its velocity, as in the sequencer
view, so softer notes are fainter. When instruments play the
same key at the same time, the lane of the key is split
between them, side by side, for as long as they overlap, so
no note hides another. A key that plays is highlighted in
the color of its instrument, as opaque as its note, and a
key that more instruments play together is highlighted
with their colors mixed, the louder notes weighing more.

\score.show pianoroll c1 a5

The sequencer view shows the bar that plays as a row of
squares for every instrument, one square for every step of
\score.seqres. Every note is a rectangle in the color of its
instrument, as long as its duration (one square for a step
of the x - notation of rhythm staffs), and as opaque as its
velocity, so softer notes are fainter. While the sequencer
plays, the step that plays is shown with a red rectangle
over all the rows, and muted instruments show no notes.

\score.show sequencer

\score.show can also take barcount, tempo, or beat, to
display the bar counter, the tempo, or a pulse on each beat.
\score.hide takes the same three arguments to hide them,
and without an argument it hides the score.

\score.show beat
\score.hide

To highlight the notes that play in the score, call:

\score.animate

and to turn this off, call \score.inanimate.
If the sequencer is not running, the animation starts with
\score.play.

\score.beattype sets how the beat pulse is drawn, 1 for a
pulse around the whole score (default), 2 for a pulse on
the playing beat of the current bar.

\score.beattype 2

\score.numbars sets how many bars are displayed (1 or more).
\score.movex and \score.movey move the score by a number of
pixels, and \score.recenter brings it back to its place.

\score.numbars 4
\score.movex -50
\score.recenter

\score.colors sets the colors of the background, the staffs
and notes, the playing notes, and the beat pulse, in this
order. Each color is a name or three RGB values (0 to 255).
The keywords invert and reset invert or reset all colors.

\score.colors black white red green
\score.colors 30 30 30 200 200 200
\score.colors reset

\score.seqres sets the resolution of the sequencer view, and
the length of a step of the x - notation of rhythm staffs,
one of 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (default), 32, or 64.

\score.seqres 8

\score.update sets when the displayed bars change when a
new loop is called, onlast (on the last bar of the current
loop) or immediately (default).

\score.update onlast

\score.correct sets whether a natural sign is added to a
note that appears earlier in the bar with an accidental
only in the same octave (onoctave, default) or in any
octave (all).

\score.correct all

An unknown method results in an error.
